  "segments": [
    {
      "id": "N01",
      "chapter": "C01",
      "heading": "The god beside the scale",
      "caption": "A famous scene of judgment",
      "sources": [
        "S04",
        "S05"
      ],
      "classification": "myth_variant",
      "visuals": [
        "hero",
        "balance"
      ],
      "text": "A heart is placed on a scale. On the other side sits a symbol of what is right and true. Standing beside the scale is a god with the head of a canine: Anubis. He is famous as the fearsome judge of the dead. But look closely at the ancient sources, and you will find that Anubis does not judge alone.",
      "word_count": 61,
      "start": 1.5,
      "end": 27.200507614213198
    },
    {
      "id": "N02",
      "chapter": "C01",
      "heading": "So what was he doing?",
      "caption": "Question: what does Anubis actually do?",
      "sources": [
        "S01",
        "S04"
      ],
      "classification": "editorial_question",
      "visuals": [
        "met_548344_detail",
        "met_544075"
      ],
      "text": "So what was Anubis actually doing? Watching over tombs, protecting the bodies of the dead, guiding them on their journey, and checking the scale that weighs the heart. In this episode, we follow sculptures and papyri at The Metropolitan Museum of Art to see, one by one, the work Anubis does in the sources.",
      "word_count": 54,
      "start": 27.700507614213198,
      "end": 50.45177664974619
    },
    {
      "id": "N03",
      "chapter": "C01",
      "heading": "Objects and reconstructions",
      "caption": "Museum photographs and reconstructions are labeled",
      "sources": [
        "S01",
        "S04",
        "S06"
      ],
      "classification": "production_method",
      "visuals": [
        "evidence",
        "met_557830"
      ],
      "text": "On screen you will see photographs of real museum objects, along with reconstructions created to help you picture the past. Every reconstruction is labeled in the top corner. Let's begin with the first object.",
      "word_count": 34,
      "start": 50.95177664974619,
      "end": 65.27664974619289
    },
    {
      "id": "N04",
      "chapter": "C02",
      "heading": "A watchful canine",
      "caption": "Lying down, but ears up",
      "sources": [
        "S01"
      ],
      "classification": "historical_fact",
      "visuals": [
        "met_544075",
        "met_544075_detail"
      ],
      "text": "First, this statue of a reclining canine. Its front legs stretch forward and its ears stand upright. It seems less asleep than alert, keeping watch. This statue at the Met is carved from limestone and was originally painted black. The god was not always shown in human form.",
      "word_count": 48,
      "start": 67.07664974619289,
      "end": 87.29999999999998
    }
  ],
